/**
 * A bare, single-purpose `variables` scope for invoking magic functions
 * (`docs/functions.bxs` - FunctionsLoader.bx) with no other members of its
 * own to collide with.
 *
 * `VariablesProcessor.bx` needs a magic function's own body to still be
 * able to bare-call a sibling function from the same `functions.bxs` (a
 * private helper, or another `$`-prefixed one), and to bare-*read* the
 * same "supporting variables" (`siteConfig`/`page`/`nav`/etc.) a `.bxm`
 * theme template already has in scope - once it's actually invoked from
 * `{{ }}` markdown, BoxLang only resolves that kind of bare reference
 * against whatever scope the call happens to run in, not something
 * captured at the function's own definition time (see FunctionsLoader.bx's
 * own docblock). Binding those directly onto VariablesProcessor's own
 * `variables` scope would make that work too, but risks a project's own
 * function name (a plain, non-`$` private helper - `$`-prefixed magic
 * functions can never collide, since none of this class's own members
 * start with `$`) silently overwriting one of VariablesProcessor's *own*
 * real methods (`resolve`, `parseArgs`, `lookupPath`, ...) for the rest of
 * the build. Routing every call through a disposable instance of *this*
 * class instead - which declares nothing but `bind()`/`call()` - removes
 * that risk entirely for the markdown path.
 *
 * (No equivalent exists for ThemeRenderer.bx's own `.bxm`-template bare
 * calls - a `.bxm` file's bare `$name(...)` has to resolve against
 * ThemeRenderer's *own* scope, since that's the instance actually
 * `include`-ing it, so that collision risk is real and documented instead
 * - see docs/guides/variables-and-functions.md's "Reserved names" section.)
 */
class {

	/**
	 * Binds every key in `values` into this instance's own `variables`
	 * scope, so one bound value (typically a function) can bare-reference
	 * another (call() below runs the same way ThemeRenderer.bx's own
	 * bind-then-bare-call does for `.bxm` templates) - `values` is
	 * FunctionsLoader.load()'s own output on one call, and the shared
	 * "supporting variables" context (`siteConfig`/`page`/`nav`/etc.) on
	 * another; both just become bare-referenceable names here, function or
	 * plain data alike.
	 *
	 * @values Struct of name -> value to bind
	 */
	void function bind( required struct values ) {
		for ( var name in arguments.values ) {
			variables[ name ] = arguments.values[ name ]
		}
	}

	/**
	 * @name A function name already bound via bind()
	 * @args Positional arguments
	 */
	any function call( required string name, required array args ) {
		return variables[ arguments.name ]( argumentCollection : arguments.args )
	}

}
